We can use a two-pointer approach. Maintain left and right pointers on the string. At each step the next character (in alphabetical order) must be at one of the two ends. Start with 'a' and greedily check if the current character is at position `lo` or `hi`. If neither matches, the answer is NO.

```cpp
bool solve(string& s) {
    int n = s.size();
    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        char c = 'a' + i;
        if (s[lo] == c) lo++;
        else if (s[hi] == c) hi--;
        else return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Time complexity: O(n). Space: O(1). This is a classic two-pointer greedy.

Greedy approach: to minimize the number of digits, always pick the largest digit available. Start from 9 and work downward. If the remaining sum is ≥ current digit, subtract it and add the digit; otherwise use the remainder.

```cpp
string solve(int x) {
    if (x > 45) return "-1";
    string res;
    for (int d = 9; d >= 1 && x > 0; d--) {
        if (x >= d) { res += ('0' + d); x -= d; }
        else { res += ('0' + x); x = 0; }
    }
    reverse(res.begin(), res.end());
    return res;
}
```

Time: O(1) since at most 9 digits. A straightforward greedy on digit selection.

If we remove two adjacent characters at positions i and i+1, the resulting string only differs from removing at positions j and j+1 when the characters at the boundaries differ. Count how many adjacent pairs have s[i] != s[i+2]. The answer is 1 + (count of such pairs).

This is essentially a sliding window of size 2 with boundary comparison.

```cpp
int solve(string& s) {
    int n = s.size(), cnt = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i + 2 < n; i++)
        if (s[i] != s[i + 2]) cnt++;
    return cnt + 1;
}
```

Time: O(n), Space: O(1).

Use a sliding window with a hash set. Expand the right pointer; if a duplicate is found, shrink from the left until the window is valid again. Track the maximum window length.

```cpp
int lengthOfLongestSubstring(string s) {
    unordered_set<char> window;
    int lo = 0, best = 0;
    for (int hi = 0; hi < (int)s.size(); hi++) {
        while (window.count(s[hi]))
            window.erase(s[lo++]);
        window.insert(s[hi]);
        best = max(best, hi - lo + 1);
    }
    return best;
}
```

Classic sliding window pattern. Time: O(n), Space: O(min(n, alphabet_size)).

Sliding window with character frequency maps. Expand right pointer until all characters in t are covered, then shrink left to find minimum. Track 'formed' count vs 'required' distinct chars.

```cpp
string minWindow(string s, string t) {
    unordered_map<char,int> need, have;
    for (char c : t) need[c]++;
    int required = need.size(), formed = 0;
    int lo = 0, best_len = INT_MAX, best_lo = 0;
    for (int hi = 0; hi < (int)s.size(); hi++) {
        have[s[hi]]++;
        if (need.count(s[hi]) && have[s[hi]] == need[s[hi]])
            formed++;
        while (formed == required) {
            if (hi - lo + 1 < best_len) {
                best_len = hi - lo + 1;
                best_lo = lo;
            }
            have[s[lo]]--;
            if (need.count(s[lo]) && have[s[lo]] < need[s[lo]])
                formed--;
            lo++;
        }
    }
    return best_len == INT_MAX ? "" : s.substr(best_lo, best_len);
}
```

This is a variable-size sliding window. Time: O(|s| + |t|). Space: O(|s| + |t|).

Use prefix sums on bracket balance. Convert '(' to +1 and ')' to -1. The prefix sum at any point tells you the nesting depth. If the total sum is nonzero, the answer is -1. Otherwise, count how many distinct 'layers' we need based on negative prefix sums.

```cpp
int solve(string& s) {
    int n = s.size();
    vector<int> prefix(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + (s[i] == '(' ? 1 : -1);
    if (prefix[n] != 0) return -1;
    int min_pref = *min_element(prefix.begin(), prefix.end());
    return min_pref < 0 ? 2 : 1;
}
```

Key insight: prefix sums convert bracket problems into range queries. Time: O(n). Space: O(n).

Use prefix sums with a hash map. For each prefix[i], we need to find how many previous prefix[j] satisfy prefix[i] - prefix[j] == k, i.e., prefix[j] == prefix[i] - k. Store prefix sum frequencies.

```cpp
int subarraySum(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<int,int> freq;
    freq[0] = 1;
    int prefix = 0, count = 0;
    for (int x : nums) {
        prefix += x;
        count += freq[prefix - k];
        freq[prefix]++;
    }
    return count;
}
```

This is the prefix sum + hash map pattern. Time: O(n), Space: O(n). Note this works with negative numbers unlike sliding window.

Binary search on the answer. We can binary search on the number of charges needed. For a given number of charges k, check if it's feasible using greedy simulation. The predicate is monotonic: if k charges suffice, then k+1 also suffice.

```cpp
bool canSend(vector<long long>& t, long long f, long long a, long long b, int charges) {
    long long cur = f;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)t.size(); i++) {
        long long diff = t[i] - (i > 0 ? t[i-1] : 0);
        cur -= diff * a;
        if (cur <= 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

long long solve(vector<long long>& t, long long f, long long a, long long b) {
    long long lo = 0, hi = 1e18;
    while (hi - lo > 1) {
        long long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (canSend(t, f, a, b, mid)) hi = mid;
        else lo = mid;
    }
    return hi;
}
```

Classic binary search on answer with a monotonic predicate. Time: O(n log C). Space: O(1).

Standard binary search for the lower bound. Find the leftmost position where target could be inserted while keeping the array sorted. This is equivalent to std::lower_bound.

```cpp
int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();
    while (lo < hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (nums[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
        else hi = mid;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

Pure binary search. Time: O(log n). Space: O(1). The loop invariant is: answer is in [lo, hi].

Maintain a Fenwick Tree (BIT) over the values. For insertions, do point update +1. For deletions by k-th smallest, binary search on the BIT prefix sums to find the k-th element, then do point update -1.

The BIT supports prefix sum queries in O(log n), and we binary search on the BIT itself to find the k-th element.

```cpp
struct BIT {
    int n;
    vector<int> tree;
    BIT(int n) : n(n), tree(n + 1, 0) {}
    void update(int i, int v) {
        for (; i <= n; i += i & (-i)) tree[i] += v;
    }
    int query(int i) {
        int s = 0;
        for (; i > 0; i -= i & (-i)) s += tree[i];
        return s;
    }
    int kth(int k) {
        int pos = 0;
        for (int pw = 1 << 20; pw; pw >>= 1)
            if (pos + pw <= n && tree[pos + pw] < k) {
                pos += pw;
                k -= tree[pos];
            }
        return pos + 1;
    }
};

void solve() {
    int n, q;
    cin >> n >> q;
    BIT bit(n);
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cin >> a[i];
        bit.update(a[i], 1);
    }
    while (q--) {
        int k; cin >> k;
        if (k > 0) bit.update(k, 1);
        else {
            int pos = bit.kth(-k);
            bit.update(pos, -1);
        }
    }
}
```

Fenwick Tree with k-th element query via binary lifting. Time: O((n+q) log n). Space: O(n).

Classic segment tree problem. Build a segment tree over the array. Point updates modify a leaf and propagate up. Range sum queries aggregate nodes covering the interval.

Alternatively, a Fenwick Tree (BIT) works here since the operation (sum) is invertible.

```cpp
class NumArray {
    int n;
    vector<int> tree;
    void build(vector<int>& a, int v, int tl, int tr) {
        if (tl == tr) { tree[v] = a[tl]; return; }
        int tm = (tl + tr) / 2;
        build(a, 2*v, tl, tm);
        build(a, 2*v+1, tm+1, tr);
        tree[v] = tree[2*v] + tree[2*v+1];
    }
    void upd(int v, int tl, int tr, int pos, int val) {
        if (tl == tr) { tree[v] = val; return; }
        int tm = (tl + tr) / 2;
        if (pos <= tm) upd(2*v, tl, tm, pos, val);
        else upd(2*v+1, tm+1, tr, pos, val);
        tree[v] = tree[2*v] + tree[2*v+1];
    }
    int qry(int v, int tl, int tr, int l, int r) {
        if (l > r) return 0;
        if (l == tl && r == tr) return tree[v];
        int tm = (tl + tr) / 2;
        return qry(2*v, tl, tm, l, min(r, tm))
             + qry(2*v+1, tm+1, tr, max(l, tm+1), r);
    }
public:
    NumArray(vector<int>& nums) : n(nums.size()), tree(4*n) {
        build(nums, 1, 0, n-1);
    }
    void update(int i, int val) { upd(1, 0, n-1, i, val); }
    int sumRange(int l, int r) { return qry(1, 0, n-1, l, r); }
};
```

Segment tree: O(n) build, O(log n) update and query. Note: both Segment Tree and Fenwick Tree solve this; Fenwick is simpler for sum but Segment Tree generalises to min/max/GCD.

Some sources claim Segment Tree queries are O(n log n) but this is incorrect — they are O(log n) per query. The build is O(n).

Use prefix sums to count inversions efficiently. Precompute prefix count of 1s and suffix count of 0s. For each element, if we flip it, recompute the inversion count contribution.

```cpp
long long solve(vector<int>& a) {
    int n = a.size();
    vector<int> pref1(n + 1, 0), suf0(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        pref1[i + 1] = pref1[i] + (a[i] == 1);
    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        suf0[i] = suf0[i + 1] + (a[i] == 0);

    long long base = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if (a[i] == 1) base += suf0[i + 1];

    long long best = base;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        long long cur;
        if (a[i] == 0)
            cur = base + suf0[i + 1] - pref1[i];
        else
            cur = base - suf0[i + 1] + pref1[i];
        best = max(best, cur);
    }
    return best;
}
```

Prefix sums enable O(1) recomputation of inversions when flipping each element. Total time: O(n).

Greedy on a tree. Every leaf must be the end of some vertical path. Start from each leaf and walk up to the root, marking visited nodes. Each contiguous unvisited chain from a leaf upward forms one path.

This is not a two-pointers or segment tree problem — it's a tree traversal.

```cpp
void solve() {
    int n; cin >> n;
    vector<int> par(n + 1);
    vector<bool> has_child(n + 1, false);
    int root = -1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        cin >> par[i];
        if (par[i] == i) root = i;
        else has_child[par[i]] = true;
    }
    vector<bool> vis(n + 1, false);
    vector<vector<int>> paths;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        if (has_child[i]) continue;
        vector<int> path;
        int v = i;
        while (!vis[v]) {
            vis[v] = true;
            path.push_back(v);
            if (v == root) break;
            v = par[v];
        }
        reverse(path.begin(), path.end());
        paths.push_back(path);
    }
    // output paths
}
```

Tree traversal from leaves, not a standard algorithmic pattern. Time: O(n).

This is a variant of Longest Non-Decreasing Subsequence, solvable with binary search + patience sorting. Maintain a sorted array of tail elements. For each element, binary search for the rightmost position where we can extend.

```cpp
vector<int> longestObstacleCourseAtEachPosition(vector<int>& obstacles) {
    vector<int> tails, res;
    for (int x : obstacles) {
        auto it = upper_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);
        int pos = it - tails.begin();
        if (it == tails.end()) tails.push_back(x);
        else *it = x;
        res.push_back(pos + 1);
    }
    return res;
}
```

Binary search on the tails array. Time: O(n log n). Space: O(n). Same technique as LIS but with upper_bound for non-decreasing.

DFS on binary tree. At each node, determine the minimum changes to redirect the path to a leaf. If the current direction is L/R and we need to go to the other child, that costs 1 change.

This is tree DP, not a standard graph pattern.

```cpp
void solve() {
    int n; cin >> n;
    string s; cin >> s; s = " " + s;
    vector<int> L(n+1), R(n+1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) cin >> L[i] >> R[i];

    // BFS/DFS to find min swaps to reach any leaf
    vector<int> dist(n + 1, 1e9);
    dist[1] = 0;
    queue<int> q;
    q.push(1);
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int v = q.front(); q.pop();
        if (L[v]) {
            int cost = (s[v] != 'L') ? 1 : 0;
            if (dist[v] + cost < dist[L[v]]) {
                dist[L[v]] = dist[v] + cost;
                q.push(L[v]);
            }
        }
        if (R[v]) {
            int cost = (s[v] != 'R') ? 1 : 0;
            if (dist[v] + cost < dist[R[v]]) {
                dist[R[v]] = dist[v] + cost;
                q.push(R[v]);
            }
        }
    }
    int ans = 1e9;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        if (!L[i] && !R[i]) ans = min(ans, dist[i]);
    cout << ans << "\n";
}
```

Tree BFS. Time: O(n). Not a standard DS pattern.

For each cell, check if it's strictly greater than all 4 neighbors. If so, replace it with the maximum neighbor value. This is a direct simulation — no advanced data structure needed.

```cpp
void solve() {
    int n, m; cin >> n >> m;
    vector<vector<int>> a(n, vector<int>(m));
    for (auto& row : a) for (auto& x : row) cin >> x;

    int dx[] = {0,0,1,-1}, dy[] = {1,-1,0,0};
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            int mx = 0;
            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                int ni = i+dx[d], nj = j+dy[d];
                if (ni >= 0 && ni < n && nj >= 0 && nj < m)
                    mx = max(mx, a[ni][nj]);
            }
            if (a[i][j] > mx) a[i][j] = mx;
        }
    // output a
}
```

Simple grid simulation. Time: O(nm). Not a standard pattern.

Use a segment tree for range maximum queries. For each element nums[i], query the segment tree for the max LIS length in range [nums[i]-k, nums[i]-1], add 1, and update position nums[i].

```cpp
struct SegTree {
    int n;
    vector<int> t;
    SegTree(int n) : n(n), t(4*n, 0) {}
    void update(int v, int tl, int tr, int pos, int val) {
        if (tl == tr) { t[v] = max(t[v], val); return; }
        int tm = (tl+tr)/2;
        if (pos <= tm) update(2*v, tl, tm, pos, val);
        else update(2*v+1, tm+1, tr, pos, val);
        t[v] = max(t[2*v], t[2*v+1]);
    }
    int query(int v, int tl, int tr, int l, int r) {
        if (l > r) return 0;
        if (l == tl && r == tr) return t[v];
        int tm = (tl+tr)/2;
        return max(query(2*v, tl, tm, l, min(r,tm)),
                   query(2*v+1, tm+1, tr, max(l,tm+1), r));
    }
};

int lengthOfLIS(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    int mx = *max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    SegTree st(mx + 1);
    int ans = 0;
    for (int x : nums) {
        int best = st.query(1, 0, mx, max(0, x-k), x-1);
        ans = max(ans, best + 1);
        st.update(1, 0, mx, x, best + 1);
    }
    return ans;
}
```

Segment tree for dynamic range max query. Time: O(n log M) where M is max value. Space: O(M). This shows segment trees going beyond simple sum queries.

Sort the array and compute prefix sums. For each starting index, binary search for the furthest right index j such that prefix[j+1] - prefix[i] <= 2 * a[i]. The answer for the query on original index maps through the sorted permutation.

```cpp
void solve() {
    int n; cin >> n;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    vector<int> idx(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { cin >> a[i]; idx[i] = i; }
    sort(idx.begin(), idx.end(), [&](int x, int y) { return a[x] < a[y]; });
    sort(a.begin(), a.end());

    vector<long long> prefix(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i+1] = prefix[i] + a[i];

    vector<int> ans(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int lo = i, hi = n - 1, best = i;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
            if (prefix[mid+1] - prefix[i] <= 2 * a[i]) {
                best = mid;
                lo = mid + 1;
            } else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        ans[i] = best;
    }
    // map answers back through idx
}
```

Prefix sums + binary search combo. Time: O(n log n). Space: O(n).

Offline sweep with sorting. Sort intervals by size and queries by value. Use a sweep line approach — but for optimal per-query answers, a sparse table or segment tree on a coordinate-compressed axis works.

Alternative: sort queries, sort intervals by left endpoint, use a priority queue to track active intervals by size.

```cpp
vector<int> minInterval(vector<vector<int>>& intervals, vector<int>& queries) {
    int n = intervals.size(), q = queries.size();
    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());
    vector<int> qi(q);
    iota(qi.begin(), qi.end(), 0);
    sort(qi.begin(), qi.end(), [&](int a, int b) { return queries[a] < queries[b]; });

    priority_queue<pair<int,int>, vector<pair<int,int>>, greater<>> pq;
    vector<int> res(q, -1);
    int j = 0;
    for (int i : qi) {
        while (j < n && intervals[j][0] <= queries[i]) {
            int len = intervals[j][1] - intervals[j][0] + 1;
            pq.push({len, intervals[j][1]});
            j++;
        }
        while (!pq.empty() && pq.top().second < queries[i]) pq.pop();
        if (!pq.empty()) res[i] = pq.top().first;
    }
    return res;
}
```

Sweep line + priority queue. Time: O((n+q) log n). An alternative approach uses a sparse table for static RMQ on the interval sizes.

We want to partition the array into contiguous subarrays and maximize the sum of (coefficient * subarray_sum). The coefficient for the i-th subarray is i. 

Key insight: using prefix sums, suffix_sum[i] = prefix[n] - prefix[i]. We add suffix_sum[i] to our answer whenever starting a new subarray at position i is beneficial (i.e., suffix_sum[i] > 0).

```cpp
long long solve(vector<int>& a) {
    int n = a.size();
    vector<long long> suffix(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        suffix[i] = suffix[i + 1] + a[i];

    long long ans = suffix[0];  // must include first segment
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
        if (suffix[i] > 0) ans += suffix[i];
    return ans;
}
```

Prefix/suffix sums with a greedy decision. Time: O(n). Space: O(n). The suffix sum tells us whether it's worth starting a new segment.
